@page "/"

<PageTitle>MVC_App Blazor client</PageTitle>

<h1>MVC_App Blazor client</h1>

<p class="lede">
    A Blazor Server front end for the MVC_App JSON API. Every CRUD action here goes out over HTTP
    to <code>/api/departments</code> or <code>/api/employees</code>, and both sides of each call
    are logged into the same <code>mvcapp-logs-*</code> indices.
</p>

<div class="card">
    <h2>What gets logged</h2>
    <p>Three separate paths, because in Blazor Server a single middleware cannot see all of them.</p>
    <table class="grid">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>What</th><th>Written by</th><th>Find it with</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Outbound API calls</td>
                <td><code>ApiLoggingHandler</code></td>
                <td><code>log_attributes.http.request.direction : "outbound"</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Inbound page requests</td>
                <td><code>RequestLoggingMiddleware</code></td>
                <td><code>log_attributes.url.path : *</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Component exceptions</td>
                <td><code>LoggingErrorBoundaryLogger</code></td>
                <td><code>log_attributes.error.boundary : true</code></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">
        Everything from this app carries <code>log_attributes.app.component : "blazor-client"</code>
        and <code>service_name : "MVC_App.BlazorClient"</code>, so it can be separated from the MVC
        app's own entries in the shared index.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Correlating a call across both apps</h2>
    <p>
        A failed call shows its trace id in the error alert. One id in Kibana returns the click,
        the outbound request, the API's own request log, its SQL, and any exception it threw:
    </p>
    <pre><code>trace_id : "paste-the-id-here"</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">
        Allow 10 to 30 seconds. The Collector batches on a 5 second timeout before Logstash picks
        the file up and Elasticsearch refreshes.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Producing errors on purpose</h2>
    <p>
        Both grids have a <strong>Simulate fault</strong> dropdown wired to the API's own
        <code>?simulate=</code> switch, which covers 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 500 and 503. The
        <a href="diagnostics">Diagnostics</a> page throws client-side failures instead, so you can
        see the difference between an API error and an exception raised inside a component.
    </p>
</div>
